<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  zedx_front.launch — bring up the front ZED X camera.

  WHY start_driver IS AN ARGUMENT
    roslaunch resolves $(find zed_wrapper) when it parses the file, and if the
    wrapper is not built the whole launch dies before anything can explain why.
    Wrapping the include in a conditional group means roslaunch never evaluates
    it when start_driver:=false, so the validation tooling can run against a
    bag or an SVO replay on a machine where zed-ros-wrapper was never built.

    zed-ros-wrapper is deliberately NOT a declared dependency in package.xml
    for the same reason — see the note there.
-->
<launch>

  <!-- Per-camera identity. Everything namespaced from this one string, so the
       rear camera can be added later by launching this file again with a
       different value rather than by editing anything. -->
  <arg name="camera_name"   default="zedx_front"/>
  <arg name="camera_model"  default="zedx"/>

  <!-- 0 = auto-detect. Set the real serial once two cameras are attached,
       otherwise which driver grabs which unit is a race at boot. -->
  <arg name="serial_number" default="0"/>

  <arg name="start_driver"  default="true"
       doc="false = configure topics only, for replaying a bag or SVO on a
            machine with no ZED SDK"/>
  <arg name="config_file"   default="$(find sidewalk_perception)/config/zedx_front.yaml"/>

  <!-- Replay: point this at a .svo2 file and the wrapper reprocesses it as if
       the camera were live, publishing the identical topic set. -->
  <arg name="svo_file"      default=""/>

  <!-- ==================== PHYSICAL MOUNTING ====================
       MEASURED 2026-08-29. These were 0.0 until then, and that was not a
       harmless default: the wrapper publishes its OWN frame tree
       (zedx_front_base_link -> camera_center -> left/right_camera_frame ->
       optical frames -> imu_link) positioned by exactly these arguments. With
       them at zero the entire tree sat on base_link, so RTAB-Map — which uses
       the wrapper's frames, not the robot URDF's — placed every observation
       0.54 m too low. Runs 1 through 6 were all recorded that way.

       z IS DERIVED FROM A TAPE MEASUREMENT, not tuned:
           0.7112   measured, floor -> ZED X housing UPPER SURFACE
         - 0.0159   half the 31.8 mm body height [datasheet Rev 1.6]
         = 0.6953   camera_center above the floor
         - 0.0160   camera base -> camera_center, from the live tree
         = 0.6793   zedx_front_base_link above the floor
         - 0.13228  base_link above the floor
         = 0.547    <- cam_pos_z

       y = -0.040 toward swiftnav_link (y = -0.426). Set 2026-08-29; it was
       +0.070. That is a PHYSICAL RE-POSITION of the camera, not a corrected
       calculation - do not "restore" the old value.

       x = 0.081 unchanged, from the mount tuned live in simulation.

       PRECISION CAVEAT: the datasheet gives the cross-section as 31.8 x 36.7 mm
       but never says which is height. 31.8 is taken as height (slim bar; 36.7
       is lens-barrel depth). Reversed, z would be 0.5446 - 2.5 mm lower. Every
       other term is measured, so that 2.5 mm is the only assumption here.

       MUST STAY IDENTICAL to zedx_base_joint in BOTH
         sidewalk_bringup/urdf/husky_a200_real.urdf   (real robot, RViz)
         sidewalk_sim/urdf/husky_real.urdf            (simulation)
       Those define the same physical mount; if they drift apart, the driver's
       frames and the robot model disagree and nothing warns you.
       robot_frames.yaml holds the LEFT LENS, which is this plus the ZED's
       internal (-0.010, +0.060, +0.016) - a different point, deliberately.

       VERIFY after any change:
           rosrun tf tf_echo zedx_base_frame zedx_front_base_link
       must read ~[0, 0, 0] - the robot model's mount and the driver's root
       landing on each other.

       PITCH IS MEASURED, 2026-08-29: 0.0565 rad = 3.24 deg NOSE DOWN.
       RANSAC plane fit to the floor in base_footprint over 5 frames, ~16500
       inliers each: -3.24 deg (spread 0.28) before, -0.03 deg (spread 0.27)
       after, against a +/-0.3 deg noise floor. Floor slope over 4 m of range
       went from 226 mm to 2 mm.

       CORRECTION, same day: an earlier version of this comment claimed
       "atan(0.226/4) = 3.23 deg confirms the normal independently". IT DOES
       NOT, and that sentence was circular. rise/4 is identically -n[0]/n[2] =
       -tan(pitch) computed from the very same fitted normal, with the plane
       offset cancelling out. It is the same number restated. This is the exact
       failure mode as the withdrawn radius finding (commit a565d31) - two
       statistics of one fit presented as two witnesses.

       Re-measuring the plane AFTER applying the correction is also not
       independent evidence. base_footprint is bolted to the robot body, not to
       the world, so the fit constrains the SUM (camera-tilt + floor-patch
       slope) both times; getting ~0 back is arithmetic confirming itself. It
       does prove the edit reached the live tree with the right sign - a
       flipped sign would have read -6.5 deg - and nothing more.

       WHAT ACTUALLY ESTABLISHED CAUSE: gravity.
           rosrun sidewalk_perception check_gravity.py _samples:=600
       The ZED X accelerometer at rest measures true vertical, which owes
       nothing to the transform tree. Through this model it lands 0.90 deg from
       vertical. Camera-level-with-sloped-floor predicts ~3.2 deg; body-pitched-
       on-its-wheels predicts ~6.5 deg. Both excluded.

       Roll left at 0: the two methods read +0.24 and -1.37 deg and disagree by
       more than either is worth. Yaw is unmeasurable by both and stays unset.

       Re-measure the angle any time with:
           rosrun sidewalk_perception measure_camera_tilt.py _frames:=5
       ...but read the caveat above before calling a clean result a
       confirmation of anything but deployment.

       The same tilt must be on zedx_base_joint's rpy in BOTH URDFs, or the
       robot model and the driver disagree again. THEY DO NOT SUM: the ZED
       driver's chain hangs off base_link directly and does not descend from
       zedx_base_frame, so the two 0.0565 values are parallel descriptions of
       one mount, not a double rotation. Verify with
           rosrun tf tf_echo zedx_base_frame zedx_front_base_link
       which must read ~[0,0,0] translation AND ~[0,0,0] rotation.
       ============================================================ -->
  <arg name="cam_pos_x"     default="0.081"/>
  <arg name="cam_pos_y"     default="-0.040"/>
  <arg name="cam_pos_z"     default="0.547"/>
  <arg name="cam_roll"      default="0.0"/>
  <arg name="cam_pitch"     default="0.0565"/>
  <arg name="cam_yaw"       default="0.0"/>

  <!-- ==================== DEPTH MODE OVERRIDE ====================
       Empty by default, which means "use whatever zedx_front.yaml says"
       (currently ULTRA). Set it ONLY for a deliberate experiment:

           roslaunch sidewalk_perception zedx_front.launch depth_mode:=NEURAL

       WHY THIS IS AN OVERRIDE AND NOT A CONFIG EDIT. ENGINEERING_NOTES.md forbids NEURAL
       as a standing setting - it costs roughly 80 % of the Orin's GPU, and this
       project deliberately moved off it (docs/SOLVED.md, "Fix zedx_front.yaml
       depth_mode NEURAL to ULTRA"). That constraint is about SUSTAINED compute
       while driving and mapping. A stationary bench measurement has no
       navigation and no SLAM at speed, so NEURAL can be evaluated there without
       contradicting the rule - but only if the change dies with the run.

       Editing the yaml instead would leave every later run silently on NEURAL,
       which is exactly how a tuning value becomes a permanent accident. This
       argument is applied AFTER the rosparam load below, so the file on disk is
       never touched and the override appears in the run's own log.

       WHATEVER IS SET HERE MUST BE RECORDED WITH THE RESULT. A depth mode that
       nobody wrote down makes the numbers unreproducible.
       ============================================================== -->
  <arg name="depth_mode" default=""
       doc="Leave empty for the configured default. NEURAL is for bench tests
            only - see the note above before using it on a driving run."/>

  <!-- ==================== EXPOSURE OVERRIDE ====================
       Same mechanism as depth_mode above, and added for the same reason: an
       experiment needs to change a setting for one run without editing a file
       that every later run reads.

       WHY IT HAS TO BE DONE HERE. Auto-exposure CANNOT be turned off at
       runtime. zed_wrapper_nodelet.cpp:2801 overwrites the incoming dynamic
       reconfigure request with the node's own internal state, and :2937 says
       outright that "Reconfigure exposure has no effect if auto_exposure_gain
       is enabled". The node reads the setting from the parameter server at
       startup instead (:1212), and it reads the TOP-LEVEL name - the config
       yaml writes video/auto_exposure_gain, which is a different parameter and
       is silently ignored. Three attempts on 2026-09-07 failed on exactly that.

       exposure and gain are percentages, 0-100, and have no effect unless
       auto_exposure is set to false.

           roslaunch sidewalk_perception zedx_front.launch                auto_exposure:=false exposure:=40 gain:=40

       WHATEVER IS SET HERE MUST BE RECORDED WITH THE RESULT, for the same
       reason as the depth mode.
       ============================================================== -->
  <arg name="auto_exposure" default=""
       doc="Empty = leave as configured. 'false' to pin exposure/gain below."/>
  <arg name="exposure" default="" doc="percent 0-100; needs auto_exposure:=false"/>
  <arg name="gain"     default="" doc="percent 0-100; needs auto_exposure:=false"/>

  <!-- ============= STEREO GEOMETRY: SELF-CALIBRATION AND STABILIZATION =====
       Added 2026-09-09, after a measured incident. Same mechanism as the two
       overrides above, and empty by default so an ordinary run is unchanged.

       WHAT SELF-CALIBRATION DOES. At every startup the SDK re-estimates the
       alignment between the two lenses and quietly adopts the result. That is
       the right behaviour for a camera on a moving robot, where a knock can
       shift the lenses - but it means the instrument can measure the same
       object differently in two sessions with nothing else changed.

       THE INCIDENT. The 4 m frame was measured at 07:47 and again at 00:29 the
       same day, both in NEURAL, with the robot verified back in place to 3.0 mm
       by the Helios. The distance between the two lower ball centres came out
       1.1120 m in the morning and 1.1543 m at night - 42 mm apart, on an object
       that had not moved and that the laser still measured at 1.0908 m. The
       left camera's focal length was unchanged to 0.006 %, the scene was fully
       lit, and the point count was within 5 %, so the change was in the stereo
       geometry itself. The frozen detector rejects any frame whose base leaves
       1.100 +/- 0.060, so 1.1543 sits 6 mm from the wall: frames were being
       discarded, the run slowed 4.3x, and the surviving samples were a
       CENSORED population whose spread would have flattered the result.

       WHY IT MATTERS BEYOND ONE RUN. A distance series compares stations
       against each other. If the instrument silently re-calibrates between
       them, a change from station to station cannot be told from a change from
       session to session. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the series
       comparable.

       depth_stabilization is here for the same reason: it averages depth over
       recent frames, so a reading depends on what came before it. 0 makes each
       frame independent. It is already pinned to 0 for offline replay, and
       live work should match.

           roslaunch sidewalk_perception zedx_front.launch \
               self_calib:=false depth_stabilization:=0

       WHATEVER IS SET HERE MUST BE RECORDED WITH THE RESULT.
       ============================================================== -->
  <arg name="self_calib" default=""
       doc="Empty = leave as configured. 'false' freezes the lens-to-lens
            geometry at the factory calibration so two sessions measure alike."/>
  <arg name="depth_stabilization" default=""
       doc="Empty = leave as configured. 0 = each frame independent."/>
  <arg name="svo_compression" default=""
       doc="Empty = leave as configured (yaml: 0 LOSSLESS, the depth study's setting - rule 16). SLAM drives: 2 = H265 lossy. Read at camera start only."/>

  <!-- ==================== CAMERA'S OWN POSITION TRACKER (SLAM drives only) ==========
       Added 2026-09-26 after three driver crashes (segmentation faults) in one night -
       camera_segfault_2026-09-26/ (project records). Empty = leave as configured,
       so the depth study's runs (rule 16) are byte-identical.
         pos_tracking_mode:=GEN_1   the older tracker engine; Stereolabs made GEN_1 the
                                    default for SDK 4.2.x "as a workaround for the random
                                    crash issue caused by GEN_2" (zed-ros2-wrapper
                                    Humble-v4.2.x release notes). The ROS 1 wrapper here
                                    still defaults to GEN_2 (common.yaml:49).
         pos_tracking:=false        tracker off. ONLY takes effect together with
                                    depth_stabilization:=0 - depth smoothing switches the
                                    tracker back on (Camera.hpp:7717; wrapper
                                    mPosTrackingRequired). Costs noisier depth (SOLVED.md,
                                    "Run 5's camera segfault": 1.73x jitter).
       Nothing in the SLAM drive stack reads the tracker's output (/zed_node/odom, /pose);
       the fused odometry uses only /zed_node/imu/data, which a separate thread publishes.
       WHATEVER IS SET HERE MUST BE RECORDED WITH THE RESULT.
       ============================================================== -->
  <arg name="pos_tracking" default=""
       doc="Empty = as configured (true). 'false' = tracker off (needs depth_stabilization:=0)."/>
  <arg name="pos_tracking_mode" default=""
       doc="Empty = as configured (wrapper default GEN_2). 'GEN_1' = older engine."/>

  <!-- ns pushes the node to /<camera_name>/zed_node/… . This wrapper build does
       NOT namespace by camera_name itself (it would publish bare /zed_node/…),
       but every perception/SLAM node in this project subscribes under
       /zedx_front/zed_node/…, so the namespace is applied here. -->
  <group if="$(arg start_driver)" ns="$(arg camera_name)">
    <include file="$(find zed_wrapper)/launch/include/zed_camera.launch.xml">
      <arg name="camera_name"   value="$(arg camera_name)"/>
      <arg name="camera_model"  value="$(arg camera_model)"/>
      <!-- NOTE: this zed-ros-wrapper build selects a unit via camera_id / the
           config yaml's general.serial_number, NOT a launch arg. Passing
           serial_number here raises "unused args [serial_number]". For two
           cameras (Phase 7), set the serial in zedx_front.yaml as
           general/serial_number rather than reintroducing it here. -->
      <arg name="svo_file"      value="$(arg svo_file)"/>
      <arg name="cam_pos_x"     value="$(arg cam_pos_x)"/>
      <arg name="cam_pos_y"     value="$(arg cam_pos_y)"/>
      <arg name="cam_pos_z"     value="$(arg cam_pos_z)"/>
      <arg name="cam_roll"      value="$(arg cam_roll)"/>
      <arg name="cam_pitch"     value="$(arg cam_pitch)"/>
      <arg name="cam_yaw"       value="$(arg cam_yaw)"/>
    </include>

    <!-- Our settings are loaded AFTER the wrapper's own defaults so they win.
         Ordering matters here: rosparam load is last-write-wins. -->
    <!-- ns="zed_node" so, inside the group ns=<camera_name>, the overrides land
         at /<camera_name>/zed_node/… — exactly where the wrapper node reads them.
         Without it the group puts them at /<camera_name>/… and the node never
         sees them (its defaults, e.g. publish_tf:true, then fight RTAB-Map's TF). -->
    <rosparam file="$(arg config_file)" command="load" ns="zed_node"/>

    <!-- AFTER the rosparam load, so it wins. rosparam load is last-write-wins,
         so ordering is the whole mechanism here. The if() keeps a normal run
         byte-identical to before: with depth_mode empty, this line does nothing
         and the yaml's value stands. -->
    <param name="zed_node/depth/depth_mode" value="$(arg depth_mode)"
           if="$(eval arg('depth_mode') != '')"/>

    <!-- Top-level names, not under video/ - see the note above. Typed, because
         an untyped "false" arrives as the STRING "false", which is truthy. -->
    <param name="zed_node/auto_exposure_gain" type="bool"
           value="$(arg auto_exposure)"
           if="$(eval arg('auto_exposure') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/exposure" type="int" value="$(arg exposure)"
           if="$(eval arg('exposure') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/gain" type="int" value="$(arg gain)"
           if="$(eval arg('gain') != '')"/>

    <!-- These two DO live under their config-file sections - general/ and
         depth/ - unlike the exposure names above. Verified against the running
         node with `rosparam get /zedx_front/zed_node/general/self_calib`, which
         is where the wrapper reads them. Typed bool/int, because an untyped
         "false" arrives as the STRING "false" and is truthy. -->
    <param name="zed_node/general/self_calib" type="bool"
           value="$(arg self_calib)"
           if="$(eval arg('self_calib') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/depth/depth_stabilization" type="int"
           value="$(arg depth_stabilization)"
           if="$(eval arg('depth_stabilization') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/general/svo_compression" type="int"
           value="$(arg svo_compression)"
           if="$(eval arg('svo_compression') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/pos_tracking/pos_tracking_enabled" type="bool"
           value="$(arg pos_tracking)"
           if="$(eval arg('pos_tracking') != '')"/>
    <param name="zed_node/pos_tracking/pos_tracking_mode" type="str"
           value="$(arg pos_tracking_mode)"
           if="$(eval arg('pos_tracking_mode') != '')"/>
  </group>

</launch>
